Relevant columns, their potential usage, and typical filter conditions
• V_ROAD."CLASS": road‐type grouping – will be grouped  
• V_ROAD."SUBCLASS": finer road classification – will be grouped  
• V_ROAD."LENGTH_M": numeric text showing length in metres – will be aggregated (CAST to NUMBER if needed)  
• V_ROAD."QUADKEY": spatial index – filter to ‘12020210’ / ‘12020211’ (e.g. WHERE "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020210%' OR "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020211%')  
• V_ROAD."GEO_CORDINATES": geometry – could be intersected with city polygons  
• V_ADMINISTRATIVE."NAMES": multilingual name object – filter city names (‘%Amsterdam%’, ‘%Rotterdam%’)  
• V_ADMINISTRATIVE."ADMIN_LEVEL": use value ‘8’ (municipality) to narrow to city polygons  
• V_ADMINISTRATIVE."GEO_CORDINATES": geometry – boundary polygons for spatial join  

Example city filters  
• Amsterdam polygon → V_ADMINISTRATIVE WHERE "ADMIN_LEVEL" = '8' AND "NAMES"::STRING ILIKE '%Amsterdam%'  
• Rotterdam polygon → V_ADMINISTRATIVE WHERE "ADMIN_LEVEL" = '8' AND "NAMES"::STRING ILIKE '%Rotterdam%'  

Example road segment filters  
• "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020210%'    -- segment family 12020210  
• "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020211%'    -- segment family 12020211  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (LIMIT 20 each) that progress from simple inspection to the full side‑by‑side aggregation.  
All column names are enclosed in double quotes and each query is annotated.

```sql
-- 1. List the administrative polygons that look like ‘Amsterdam’ (municipality level)
SELECT 
    "ID",
    "ADMIN_LEVEL",
    "SUBCLASS",
    "NAMES",
    "GEO_CORDINATES"
FROM NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ADMINISTRATIVE
WHERE "ADMIN_LEVEL" = '8'
  AND "NAMES"::STRING ILIKE '%Amsterdam%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. List the administrative polygons that look like ‘Rotterdam’ (municipality level)
SELECT 
    "ID",
    "ADMIN_LEVEL",
    "SUBCLASS",
    "NAMES",
    "GEO_CORDINATES"
FROM NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ADMINISTRATIVE
WHERE "ADMIN_LEVEL" = '8'
  AND "NAMES"::STRING ILIKE '%Rotterdam%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. See distinct road classes present in the two requested QUADKEY segment families
SELECT DISTINCT 
    "CLASS"
FROM NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ROAD
WHERE "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020210%' 
   OR "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020211%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. See distinct (class, subclass) pairs for those segment families
SELECT DISTINCT 
    "CLASS",
    "SUBCLASS"
FROM NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ROAD
WHERE "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020210%' 
   OR "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020211%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Quick sample of road records in QUADKEY 12020210 family
SELECT 
    "ID",
    "CLASS",
    "SUBCLASS",
    "LENGTH_M",
    "QUADKEY"
FROM NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ROAD
WHERE "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020210%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Sum total length by class inside QUADKEY 12020210 family
SELECT 
    "CLASS",
    SUM(TRY_TO_NUMBER("LENGTH_M")) AS "TOTAL_LENGTH_M"
FROM NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ROAD
WHERE "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020210%'
GROUP BY "CLASS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Sum total length by class inside QUADKEY 12020211 family
SELECT 
    "CLASS",
    SUM(TRY_TO_NUMBER("LENGTH_M")) AS "TOTAL_LENGTH_M"
FROM NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ROAD
WHERE "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020211%'
GROUP BY "CLASS"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Sum length for every (class, subclass) pair for 12020210 and 12020211 separately
SELECT 
    "CLASS",
    "SUBCLASS",
    CASE WHEN "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020210%' THEN '12020210' ELSE '12020211' END AS "SEGMENT_FAMILY",
    SUM(TRY_TO_NUMBER("LENGTH_M")) AS "TOTAL_LENGTH_M"
FROM NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ROAD
WHERE "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020210%' 
   OR "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020211%'
GROUP BY 
    "CLASS",
    "SUBCLASS",
    CASE WHEN "QUADKEY" LIKE '12020210%' THEN '12020210' ELSE '12020211' END
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. (Optional) Sample intersection test: pick one Amsterdam polygon and see 20 roads that fall inside it
SELECT 
    r."ID",
    r."CLASS",
    r."SUBCLASS",
    r."LENGTH_M"
FROM
    (SELECT "GEO_CORDINATES" AS "GEOM"
     FROM NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ADMINISTRATIVE
     WHERE "ADMIN_LEVEL" = '8'
       AND "NAMES"::STRING ILIKE '%Amsterdam%'
     LIMIT 1) a,
    NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ROAD r
WHERE ST_INTERSECTS(a."GEOM", r."GEO_CORDINATES")
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final side‑by‑side total length (in metres) by class & subclass for Amsterdam vs Rotterdam,
--     limited to roads whose QUADKEY starts with 12020210 or 12020211
SELECT
    agg."CLASS",
    agg."SUBCLASS",
    SUM(CASE WHEN agg."CITY" = 'Amsterdam'  THEN agg."SEG_LEN" END) AS "AMSTERDAM_LENGTH_M",
    SUM(CASE WHEN agg."CITY" = 'Rotterdam'  THEN agg."SEG_LEN" END) AS "ROTTERDAM_LENGTH_M"
FROM (
    SELECT
        r."CLASS",
        r."SUBCLASS",
        TRY_TO_NUMBER(r."LENGTH_M") AS "SEG_LEN",
        CASE
            WHEN a."NAMES"::STRING ILIKE '%Amsterdam%' THEN 'Amsterdam'
            WHEN a."NAMES"::STRING ILIKE '%Rotterdam%' THEN 'Rotterdam'
        END AS "CITY"
    FROM NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ROAD r
    JOIN NETHERLANDS_OPEN_MAP_DATA.NETHERLANDS.V_ADMINISTRATIVE a
      ON ST_INTERSECTS(a."GEO_CORDINATES", r."GEO_CORDINATES")
    WHERE a."ADMIN_LEVEL" = '8'
      AND (a."NAMES"::STRING ILIKE '%Amsterdam%' OR a."NAMES"::STRING ILIKE '%Rotterdam%')
      AND (r."QUADKEY" LIKE '12020210%' OR r."QUADKEY" LIKE '12020211%')
) agg
GROUP BY 
    agg."CLASS",
    agg."SUBCLASS"
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries let you (1) find the relevant city boundaries, (2) explore the road data in the required QUADKEY families, and (3) ultimately compute a side‑by‑side comparison of the total road lengths for Amsterdam and Rotterdam, broken down by each combination of road class and subclass.
